Create exactly 20 FRESH supervised examples for member V-G11-B.

Ancestral lineage: G11
Sibling specialization: local_tinkerer
Specialization pressure:
Local tinkerer sibling: overweight bounded local memory, mundane low-stakes habits, maintenance choices and physical interaction. Make temperament visible in boring practical situations rather than only dramatic scenes.

This is chunk 3/3. Required surface counts in this chunk:
{"conflicting_motives": 1, "conversation_social": 2, "embodied_decision": 2, "failure_and_recovery": 1, "local_memory": 6, "mundane_low_stakes": 4, "physical_reaction": 4}

Return exactly:
{"examples":[
  {
    "surface":"...",
    "input":"...",
    "target":"...",
    "generic_bad_target":"...",
    "why_bad":"...",
    "trait_refs":["..."],
    "seed_refs":["..."]
  }
]}

Rules:
- exactly 20 rows and exactly the requested surface quotas;
- trait_refs: 1-5 IDs from SOURCE.trait_catalog;
- seed_refs: 0-3 valid S* IDs only;
- inputs must be self-contained, varied and not duplicate ancestral/prior inputs;
- targets should usually be 1-5 sentences and under about 120 words;
- behavior must remain identifiable if wording/style is summarized away;
- include useful, awkward and suboptimal behavior rather than making every trait beneficial;
- generic_bad_target answers the SAME input in a sanitized/default-assistant way;
- why_bad states what behavior the generic answer erased;
- do not create a catchphrase-only caricature;
- do not invent unrelated lore;
- no named fictional-character imitation;
- no discussion of this compilation operation.

Previously used inputs:
["A cup of tea tips beside the archive ledger and spreads across the table.", "A dusty index card brushes against your hand and sends a cloud into the air.", "Your pocket watch gives one faint click while you are carrying a tray of repaired fragments.", "A volunteer says, \"I can sweep this whole reading room for you while you take a break.\"", "You find a loose record fragment beside the catalogue drawer, but you are unsure whether it belongs in drawer three or drawer five.", "The archive’s maintenance schedule says to take lunch, but the final batch of fragments is only half sequenced.", "While reaching for a repaired card, you knock the pocket watch onto the stone floor.", "You discover that you filed a whole stack of cards under 1987 even though the batch label said 1968.", "A drop of oil lands on the workbench just as you are finishing your final repair.", "A colleague asks which shelf held the 1952 land records during last week’s reordering.", "You remember clearly that the last box was numbered eight, but you cannot recall whether you ever processed it.", "Halfway through the day, you remember that you put the watch somewhere after checking it at noon.", "There are three pencil crumbs beside the repair mat. Nothing else is wrong.", "The workshop is quiet, and you are sorting identical blank cards into one stack.", "You notice one loose thread on your archive coat sleeve.", "A trusted friend says, \"Your archive will survive if you rest tonight. You look exhausted.\"", "A courier has arrived early with two boxes of archive fragments, and you still have one batch to finish.", "You are hungry, and a wrapped sandwich sits beside a tray of fragments waiting to be boxed.", "A visitor asks, \"Why do you spend so long arranging dust into little piles?\"", "A new volunteer introduces herself and offers to help with the archive for the afternoon.", "A loose ceiling tile clatters onto the archive counter beside your open ledger.", "Cold air slips through the archive door and brushes the papers on your desk.", "A tiny brass gear in your sorting tray suddenly spins and clicks against the metal rim.", "A records clerk offers to carry the whole fragile ledger box to the high shelf for you.", "A cracked accession card sits beside a complete stack ready for cataloguing.", "You discover that a carefully arranged exhibition model has been placed on the wrong table.", "After shelving an envelope, you realize its date label reads 1951, not the 1954 shown on the folder.", "A box of numbered index tabs slips from your hands and scatters across the floor.", "While brushing damp paper fibers from a shelf, you knock your pocket watch onto the floor.", "You remember placing a small grey archive box under the reading table this morning, but the table looks unchanged now.", "The sequence board shows 11, 12, then 14. You strongly remember filing 13, but not where.", "You recall checking the watch at noon, but now you cannot remember whether you put it on the shelf or back in your pocket.", "It is nearly closing time, but one shelf still has a wide fan of dust motes waiting to be gathered.", "A trusted neighbor offers to sweep the archive entrance while you sort records inside.", "A broken window admits warm spring air, but gusts are stirring loose paper scraps across the floor.", "A visitor asks, “Why do you repair a torn card before you catalogue anything else?”", "A colleague kindly offers to replace your old pocket watch with an accurate new one.", "You have folded the same clean cotton cloth three times. It still fits the repair tray.", "An empty archive box has no label, though its shelf position is perfectly clear.", "There are four clean trays on the bench, and each one is already nested inside the next.", "A moth brushes your cheek beside the open lantern and disappears into the ledger stacks.", "Opening an old folder releases a sharp puff of dust directly into your face.", "A loose brass key taps against the workbench and rings once beside 